{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"EPIC CLAIMED and extended with a seventh story (2026-09-03). Recording the operator decisions on the ticket itself so they do not live only in the session log (crystal-parodistic-boar / ba7f-f1c0-20e7-4dbc).\n\n## New story: S6 grusome-ecumenical-polarbear (09da-343c-1ee9-480c)\n\n\"Fast-fail gate admission above a configurable concurrency cap.\" Added on operator input that ~10 plan reviews run in parallel routinely, plus code reviews and autodeploy contending for the same volume.\n\nWHY IT IS LOAD-BEARING, not additive: Option A caps each accumulator via a high-water-mark GC, and such a GC assumes evictable data exists. src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py documents that it relies on POSIX delete-on-last-close plus touch-on-read mtime recency and deliberately takes NO per-reader lease — a PID+heartbeat lease was spiked and REJECTED as unsound (N readers per entry, PID reuse, crash-stale leases), following Gitaly/Sourcegraph/Bazel/ccache — and it skips entries inside a grace window. So the janitor may UNLINK an in-flight snapshot, but the blocks are not returned until the last reader closes.\n\nUnder ~10 concurrent reviews the held bytes are therefore LIVE, not garbage. A cap set below peak concurrent hold cannot be honoured: the GC runs, finds nothing evictable, and the volume fills anyway. That is the same shape as the failure this epic exists to fix — bug 3276 measured prune's reclaimable ceiling at ~1.06 GB against a 29 GB problem.\n\nWithout S6, S2-S5's caps are aspirational under exactly the load that causes the incident. S6 therefore BLOCKS S2, S3, S4 and S5 (edges added), and is itself blocked by S0.\n\nDESIGN (operator-specified): one configurable limit shared by plan-review AND completion-verifier, since both create a copy of the repo at a ref and contend for the same disk. At capacity the call FAST-FAILS with a clear \"try again later\" congestion message rather than queueing, so clients can modulate their own load. Fast-fail over queueing is deliberate — a queued gate still holds its thread and ~739 MB of resident memory while waiting, so a queue converts disk pressure into memory pressure. The rejection must be distinguishable from a gate verdict so an agent does not read congestion as INDETERMINATE or as a failed review.\n\n## Cap values (operator decision)\n\nDerive each cap from measurement as a sensible DEFAULT, and make it settable via a config value — not a hardcoded constant. Grounding available: root working set at the outage was 28G — /var/lib/docker 17G (overlay2 16G across 67 layer dirs), /var/tmp 3.6G, /var/log 1.8G (journal 1.7G) — with ~6.5 GB of that orphaned overlay2 invisible to prune. Note each new config key trips the shrink-only mechanism ratchet and needs an in-tree justification marker naming its ticket, never a baseline edit.\n\n## Tree shape — this lands as ONE STACK\n\nS2-S5 are NOT file-disjoint: all four touch infra/scripts/observability.sh, infra/scripts/compose-up.sh and infra/scripts/install-disk-ceilings.sh. Parallel worktrees are only licensed for genuinely disjoint leaves, so the seven stories land as a single ordered Gerrit stack. The plan-review gate independently reached the same conclusion (advisory P5: \"sibling tickets touch the same file(s) with no ordering edge\").\n\n## Three gate advisories the implementation must honour\n\n1. Epic AC4 (\"existing root/data disk alarms remain present; the cyclopean missing-data fix is not regressed\") is covered by NO child story's acceptance criteria. It needs an explicit check in whichever leaf lands last, or its own criterion.\n2. S2 and S5 overlap: writable container layers live IN overlay2, so their caps interact and must be designed as one budget rather than two independent ones.\n3. S4's /var/tmp cap conflicts with sibling task 3e92 (scowlful-bovine-dragonfly), which documents /var/tmp/rebar-evidence/<ticket>-<stamp>/ as the trap-cleaned evidence location. Capping /var/tmp must not evict evidence mid-investigation.\n\n## Attestation note\n\nThe epic's plan-review needed two runs: the first returned INDETERMINATE with zero blocking findings, cause llm-degrade NEEDS_INVESTIGATION (LLM infrastructure, not a plan defect — prior tickets exist for the same condition on completion-verify). The retry PASSED and signed at head_sha ebbcd7a030d6029216127c83fb43c415117af366."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788478767177420001,"uuid":"a91999e1-85a2-40f9-9494-fa409edd9914"}